The question is whether this has a major influence on the probabilities.
 It depends on the relative number of potions created this way and the
"ordinary" way.

> Shopkeepers (75% have healing, 50% have extra healing as well)

Shopkeepers are an interesting case.  For one thing, I would count their
potions as largely inaccessible in many games.  As a lawful or neutral,
I do refrain from killing shopkeepers (more so, I must admit in slashem
than in nethack); second, most ways to kill them fast enough so they
will not be able to drink their potions are costly in one way or
another.  (For example, I would not be willing to spend charges of a
wand of death on them, unless I really needed those potions desperately.
Most of the time, I will, however, not even have such a wand before the
castle. An Archon pet would do, I suppose, but I usually don't get that
either before the castle.)

Otherwise, these additional potions may skew things quite a bit, that's
true.  We have three to four shops in mine town and, estimating again,
the same number on average in the rest of the dungeon, maybe even
slightly more.  So assume we have eight shops, which makes 6 additional
potions of healing and 4 of extra healing.  This means 80 almost
guaranteed additional HP, if you are willing and capable of killing
those shopkeepers fast enough.  If you are not capable of doing so
(which may be the case before the castle) then, I think, the difference
between generated and accessible potions *is* important here.

