In any case, all that is needed to do a correct calculation is the true
effective probability of generation of the potions we are talking about,
rolling all the effects you mention into one number, and the true
average number of potions found per level. If this is evaluated from
about 1000 games, I think one can make pretty reliable statements.  What
your discussion suggests, then, is that these true probabilities are far
higher for *healing potions than given nominally (unless the high
abundance of object detection does not reduce them back to their
original value :-) ).

